      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The defect-mediated account of two-dimensional melting and the hexatic phase is a clear, general statistical-physics mechanism that changed how phase transitions in low-dimensional systems are understood.",
        "attribution": "Halperin and Nelson developed the central theory, with Young making a key contribution to its formulation; all three are credible recipients for the work as described.",
        "maturity": "The theory is longstanding and has been tested against experiments and later work on two-dimensional systems.",
