        "Christopher Jarzynski",
        "Gavin E. Crooks",
        "Denis J. Evans"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Exact nonequilibrium work relations extend thermodynamic reasoning to fluctuating systems and have had lasting influence in statistical physics and molecular-scale experiments.",
        "attribution": "Jarzynski and Crooks established influential exact relations, while Evans's earlier fluctuation-theorem work supplies an important foundation; the three-name credit is plausible for this combined discovery.",
        "maturity": "The results have been tested in experiments and simulations and are now standard tools in nonequilibrium statistical physics.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The candidate groups several related results, and the relative priority and distinct contributions of the credited researchers need careful separation."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B036",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Yakir Aharonov",
        "Michael Berry"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov–Bohm effect and geometric phase reveal that quantum phases have observable consequences beyond local classical forces, with broad influence across quantum physics.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov is central to the first effect and Berry to the geometric phase; both contributions are distinct and foundational, though the candidate combines them.",
        "maturity": "Both ideas have been verified in many settings and are deeply integrated into modern quantum theory.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The two discoveries are related but distinct, so treating them as one prize contribution requires a clear account of the connection."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
